    <h2>1. The Physics of Sprinkler Distribution Profiles (The Wedge Profile)</h2>
    <p>
      If you set a line of catch-cups across the spray radius of a single pop-up rotor or spray head, you will discover that water deposition is not flat. A properly engineered sprinkler nozzle produces a <strong>tapered wedge distribution profile</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Base of the Head (0 to 25% of radius):</strong> Highest water precipitation volume directly adjacent to the nozzle body.</li>
      <li><strong>Mid-Stream (25% to 75% of radius):</strong> Steady, linear decline in water volume as the stream spreads over an expanding arc.</li>
      <li><strong>Outer Perimeter (75% to 100% of radius):</strong> Very sparse mist and scattered droplet impact, applying only 15% to 25% of the water volume recorded near the head.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If you place two heads 30 feet apart, and each head has a 15-foot throw radius, their tips will touch in the center, but that central turf receives only 20% + 20% = 40% of the water required to keep turf alive. The grass in the middle burns brown every summer.
    </p>
    <p>
      Under the <strong>100% head-to-head overlap rule</strong>, each sprinkler head is positioned to throw water completely across the gap until its stream hits the base of the adjacent sprinkler head. When the declining taper of Head A overlaps with the declining taper of Head B, their combined precipitation sums to an exceptionally uniform 100% distribution curve across the entire turf surface.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Head Spacing Geometry: Square vs Triangular Patterns</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-700 uppercase font-semibold">
