            <th class="p-3 border-b">Layout Spacing Formula</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Best Turf Application</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Wind Resistance</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Distribution Uniformity</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 bg-white">
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Square Spacing</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-blue-700 font-bold">Spacing = 1.0 x Radius (S = R)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Rectangular yards, fence lines &amp; driveways</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Moderate (&le; 5 MPH winds)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">75% – 82% Christiansen Uniformity</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Equilateral Triangular Spacing</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Spacing = 1.0 x Radius; Row = 0.866 x S</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Large open lawns, irregular curves &amp; parks</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">High (Excellent in gusty areas)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">88% – 94% Christiansen Uniformity</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>3. Matched Precipitation Rates (MPR): The Arc-to-Flow Law</h2>
    <p>
      A common amateur mistake is installing identical 2.0 GPM nozzles on every head in a zone regardless of whether the head rotates 90 degrees (quarter-circle corner), 180 degrees (half-circle border), or 360 degrees (full-circle center).
    </p>
    <p>
      Consider what happens when a 2.0 GPM nozzle waters a 90-degree corner versus a 360-degree circle:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A <strong>quarter-circle head</strong> sweeps across its 90-degree arc in 15 seconds, returning to re-water the same square footage 4 times per minute.</li>
      <li>A <strong>full-circle head</strong> requires 60 seconds to sweep 360 degrees, watering that same square footage only once per minute.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If both heads discharge 2.0 GPM, the corner head applies <strong>400% more water per square foot</strong> than the center head! The corner turns into a muddy, algae-covered swamp while the center of the lawn wilts from drought.
    </p>
    <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-600 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-blue-900 my-4">
      Matched Precipitation Golden Rule:<br/>
      • 90° Corner Nozzle = 1 x GPM (e.g., 0.5 GPM)<br/>
      • 180° Half-Circle Nozzle = 2 x GPM (e.g., 1.0 GPM)<br/>
      • 270° Three-Quarter Nozzle = 3 x GPM (e.g., 1.5 GPM)<br/>
      • 360° Full-Circle Nozzle = 4 x GPM (e.g., 2.0 GPM)
    </div>

    <h2>4. Sizing Zones by GPM Capacity: The 80% Rule and Friction Loss</h2>
    <p>
      To size an irrigation zone, follow this 3-step hydraulic calculation:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Determine Dynamic Supply Flow:</strong> Measure your home's water flow at the outdoor sillcock using a bucket and stopwatch. For example, if a 5-gallon bucket fills in 25 seconds: (5 gal / 25 sec) x 60 sec = <strong>12.0 GPM</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Apply the 80% Safety Factor:</strong> Never budget 100% of available flow. Municipal pressure fluctuates when neighbors run appliances, and friction losses inside valves and backflow preventers siphon off pressure. 12.0 GPM x 0.80 = <strong>9.6 GPM Maximum Safe Zone Budget</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Sum Individual Nozzle Demands:</strong> Add up the nozzle flows for your heads on that zone until you reach 9.6 GPM. If you are using 1.2 GPM rotary nozzles, your maximum head count for that zone is: 9.6 GPM / 1.2 GPM = <strong>8 Heads Maximum</strong>.</li>
    </ol>

