    title="Why Water Velocity Above 5 Feet Per Second Will Shatter Your Underground Fittings"
    note="When selecting lateral pipe diameter for an irrigation zone, many DIYers run 1/2-inch poly pipe to save $15 on a 100-foot roll. This is a severe hydraulic blunder. Under the Hazen-Williams hydraulic flow formula, pushing 10 GPM through a 1/2-inch pipe forces water velocity to exceed 12 feet per second (FPS). The Irrigation Association and International Plumbing Code limit fluid velocity in plastic piping to a maximum of 5.0 FPS. When water moving at 12 FPS suddenly hits a fast-closing solenoid valve, kinetic energy converts instantly into hydraulic shock (water hammer), generating transient pressure spikes exceeding 250 PSI. These violent pressure shockwaves shatter PVC slip fittings, rip solenoid diaphragms, and split polyethylene tubing right at the barbs. Always run 1-inch pipe for your mainlines and lateral feeds, restricting 1/2-inch tubing solely to short 12-inch to 18-inch flexible swing pipe drops directly beneath individual sprinkler heads."
  />

